  "source_text": "Chapter Forty Seven.\n\nAt seven on Sunday morning, Adrian's encrypted terminal received a notification it should not have been able to receive.\n\nHe was in Berlin, in Ines Brandt's factory space in Kreuzberg, going over final integration plans with the Scaffold herself. The factory, a converted industrial building that Ines had transformed into something between a server farm and a greenhouse, with racks of humming equipment standing alongside inexplicable clusters of potted ferns, was the closest thing the movement had to a central nervous system. Not because it housed the AI, but because Ines's distribution platform would be the vehicle for everything Project Lumen released.\n\nThey were arguing. This was normal. Ines argued the way other people breathed: constantly, naturally, and with no apparent effort. She was currently explaining, with the patient intensity of a schoolteacher addressing a particularly slow student, why Adrian's proposed data routing architecture was \"structurally naive.\"\n\n\"You're thinking in pipelines,\" she said, jabbing a finger at his screen. \"Data goes in here, comes out there, like water through a hose. That's not how distributed publishing works. That's not how anything works if you want it to survive contact with reality. You need to think in networks. In ecosystems. In —\"\n\nThe notification arrived.\n\nIt appeared simultaneously on every active device in the factory. Adrian's terminal. Ines's workstation. The monitoring displays mounted on the walls. Even the tablet that Ines kept by the coffee maker for reading the news during her three-minute coffee breaks.\n\nEvery screen displayed the same message, in red text, in a font size that was clearly designed for urgency:\n\nSHIELD COMPROMISED. BREACH IN PROGRESS. ALL ENCRYPTED CHANNELS CONSIDERED HOSTILE. INITIATE PROTOCOL OMEGA.\n\nInes was already moving. Whatever her opinions about Adrian's data architecture, the woman could switch from debate mode to crisis mode faster than anyone he'd ever seen. Her fingers flew across her workstation, pulling up system monitors, network diagnostics, the real-time status of the AI's two hundred and fourteen relay nodes.\n\n\"It's the AI,\" she said. Her voice was flat. Controlled. The voice of someone who had war-gamed this scenario and was now executing the plan she'd hoped she'd never need. \"Someone's inside the encryption layer. They're reading everything.\"\n\n\"How?\"\n\n\"I don't know how. The question isn't how. The question is how much.\"\n\nHer monitoring displays answered that question in real time, and the answer was: everything.\n\nNode by node, the AI's network was being peeled open. Adrian watched the visualization, the same kind of map Ólafur had shown him in Iceland, the web of interconnected nodes pulsing with data, and saw the nodes change color. Blue for secure. Yellow for compromised. Red for fully breached. The colors were spreading across the map like an infection, starting from the European nodes and radiating outward.\n\n\"They're decrypting our communications,\" Ines said. \"Not just current. Archived. Everything the AI has managed for the past two years. Every message, every file transfer, every member identification handshake.\"\n\nThe implications hit Adrian like a physical force. Every message meant every name. Every person who had ever communicated through the AI's protected channels, from Seb at the top to the lowest-level cell members in San Francisco, was being identified, located, and logged by whoever was conducting the breach.\n\nHis phone rang. Seb.\n\n\"Tell me you're seeing this.\" Seb's voice was steady, but the steadiness was the kind that came from effort rather than calm.\n\n\"I'm seeing it.\"\n\n\"It's Strutt. Has to be. His surveillance teams. They found the AI and they're cracking it open like an egg.\" A pause. \"Adrian, the member database is in there. Every person who was recruited through the AI's channels. Four hundred and twelve people.\"\n\nFour hundred and twelve names. Four hundred and twelve addresses. Four hundred and twelve people who had trusted that their identities were protected by a system that was, at this moment, being disassembled by the most sophisticated surveillance apparatus in the private sector.\n\n\"Can we shut it down?\" Adrian asked. \"Kill the AI? If the system goes dark —\"\n\n\"Then the data that's already been extracted is still in their hands, and we lose our protective cover permanently instead of temporarily. Ines?\"",
